{% block how_it_works %}
## How it works

The two arms are sets of paths: `diff` is resolved per path, and a path that
would fall in both arms raises an error instead of being counted twice. With an
`assignment` table the population is intention-to-treat — every assigned path,
including those with no events (they count as zeros), with events before
`assigned_at` dropped. Without it the population is the stream's paths, each
with at least one event. The `population` column says which (`"assignment"` or
`"stream"`; `"active"` on `period_population="active"` rows, which compare the
survivors of a period), so a funnel's rate on the stream can be told apart from
the ITT rate. A metric that has a value only for some paths (`time_between`,
`column_mean`, a ratio such as order value) compares those paths, not the whole
arms: `group1_support` / `group2_support` count them and `conditional` flags
the row.

The path grain must be an independent analysis unit compatible with randomisation. The
scorecard, bootstrap, per-path table and sample-ratio check require the coarsest declared
path grain for every diff form, even with an assignment table or a small sample. Funnel
and conversion intervals use the same rule. A finer-grain assignment table cannot establish
independence between nested paths. For genuinely independent session-level designs, declare
sessions as the primary schema grain; changing schema order alone cannot repair dependence.
Ordinary metrics and point-only funnels remain available at finer grains.

Known binary outcomes default to Fisher exact inference, with Clopper–Pearson
arm intervals and a conservative difference formed by splitting alpha across the
arms. That difference is not a Fisher-test inversion. Nonbinary outcomes default
to descriptive estimates, without p-values, intervals or MDE. Approximate Welch
and whole-unit bootstrap require explicit nonbinary requests; known-binary Welch
and scorecard bootstrap reject. Explicit binary z inference requires at least
five pooled-null expected successes and failures per arm and remains approximate.
Relative intervals are withheld on the exact Fisher path.

The verdict reads the adjusted p-value; intervals are per comparison unless an
available Bonferroni interval is requested. Holm includes all declared rows,
including unavailable hypotheses. BH requires independence or PRDS. Separate
calls and repeated looks are not automatically corrected. MDE, when available,
is labeled `normal_approximation`, not actual Fisher-test power. See the
[supported inference boundary](/docs/ab-testing#supported-inference).

### Assignment ids and time

Table ids are matched to `path_col` as they are, or by their string form when
none matches as is (ids read from JSON or CSV). An unnamed table with no matches raises
`InvalidParameterError`. A table explicitly indexed by `path_col` may validly
contain only silent assigned units; it warns, preserves them and returns no-data
rows. Check id spelling before interpreting this as silence; fewer than half of the table's ids matched gives a
`UserWarning` with the counts — right when most assigned paths never produced an
event, wrong when the ids are spelled differently. A table whose index is named
after a coarser path column (user ids, with `path_col="session_id"`) or after a
finer one (`session_id` read per user) raises; an unnamed index whose ids are
more often ids of a finer column warns.

A naive `assigned_at` on a tz-aware stream is read in the stream's timezone: a
wall time skipped by a daylight-saving change moves forward, and one that occurs
twice (the hour the clocks go back) raises — pass a tz-aware `assigned_at`. A
tz-aware one is converted to the stream's timezone; on a naive stream it raises.

### Periods and windows

Calendar periods (`period_basis="calendar"`) are bins of the event timestamp in
the stream's timezone: `"D"`, `"W"`, `"M"`, `"Q"`, `"Y"` (`"W-MON"` etc. pick the
week's end; `"ME"`, `"MS"`, `"QE"`, `"QS"`, `"YE"`, `"YS"` and
`pd.offsets.MonthEnd()` mean the same as their unit), a multiple of weeks,
months, quarters or years (`"2W"`, `"3M"`, counted from the unit that holds the
stream's first day; another unit with a multiple raises), or a fixed length
(`"7D"`, `"12h"`) whose bins start at midnight of the first day. A period's
population is every path assigned before its end (`assigned_at`, or the path's
first event without it; silent paths with no `assigned_at` are in every
period). `period_complete` is False when the observation window cuts the period. With an
explicit cutoff that window ends at `observation_end`; otherwise the observed
event span supplies only descriptive calendar coverage.

Since-assignment bins (`period_basis="since_assignment"`, a fixed length:
`"7D"`, `"14D"`, `"W"` = 7 days) are labelled `"0-6"`, `"7-13"` (days) or
`"0-11h"` (hours). A path is in a bin's population only when the bin's window
closes by the explicit `observation_end`; the others are counted in `immature_paths`, and a bin
with no such path in either arm is left out. A bin that `horizon` cuts
(`horizon="10D"` with `period="7D"`: `"7-13"` holds days 7 to 9) keeps its label
with `period_complete=False`. A bare number (`14`) has no unit and raises for
`period` and `horizon` alike. Both `horizon` and since-assignment periods require
`observation_end`, an exclusive timestamp asserting complete logging through that
time. Events at or after the cutoff are excluded. Do not substitute the last
observed event: inactivity does not establish when observation ended. Rows report
`observation_end` and `observation_basis` so the window is auditable.

### Support and coverage

`min_support` is pooled over the two arms and counted in paths: paths with the
event for 0/1 metrics (and, separately, the paths without it), paths with a
non-zero total for totals (every path has a total, so "a value" would be every
path), paths with a non-zero numerator for ratios, and paths with a value for
values. Below it a row is `insufficient_data`: the estimates stay, the test does
not run. Because support is pooled, a behaviour present in one arm only — a new
feature's event, 0 paths in control — is tested and flagged `one_arm_only`. A
value or ratio metric with no value in an arm has no estimate there and is
`insufficient_data` with `support_warning="no_data"`, as is a row where no path
of either arm has the behaviour.

Percentile bootstrap intervals under-cover with few supporting paths. Simulated
under a true null with 300 paths per arm, the 95 % interval covered about 0.93
at 60 supporting paths per arm; at about 6 per arm (2 % of paths with an order)
a ratio row rejected 6 % of the time and covered 0.91. Bootstrap rows below 30
supporting paths in an arm keep their verdict and are flagged `low_support`.
Any replicate without a value in an arm (a ratio's denominator at
0) gives `invalid_replicates`: no interval, p-value, `delta_se` or `mde`. Undefined draws are not discarded before computing
quantiles, since that would condition the interval on the statistic being defined.
Degenerate bootstrap distributions also cannot provide an inferential verdict.

An absent estimate is NaN, never 0. `p_value` is unavailable for degenerate approximate inference (equal
constant nonempty binary arms instead have Fisher p=1), when an arm has fewer than two paths with a value (totals and values),
and on `insufficient_data`, `descriptive` and `invalid_replicates` rows (a
`low_support` row keeps it); `delta_se` and `mde` are NaN when the metric has no
spread; `rel_delta*` are NaN when `group2_value <= 0`; an arm with no path has
NaN estimates; `heterogeneity_*` are NaN with fewer than two strata that pass
the support guard (>= 5 events and >= 5 non-events per arm for 0/1 metrics, >= 10
supporting paths per arm otherwise).

Strata without an explicit pre-treatment declaration are descriptive: they retain estimates and
intervals, but no p-values, MDE, balance or heterogeneity test. An exploratory flag
is not permission to interpret post-treatment selection causally.

### Reading a row

`reading` reports the window, population, effect, method, availability and
correction from that row. It does not verify randomization, complete logging or
the analyst's causal declarations. Inspect SRM status and design independently.
An inconclusive test does not prove no effect; a confidence interval is not a
posterior probability statement. `mde_basis="normal_approximation"` identifies a
planning approximation, not actual power for Fisher's test.

Default Fisher/descriptive strata have `strata_diagnostics_basis="not_requested"`
and no balance, heterogeneity or stratum-versus-rest p-values. Explicit supported
approximate methods expose guarded `"asymptotic_exploratory"` diagnostics outside
the primary corrected family. Truncation or transferred metadata never certifies
pre-treatment labels; reassert the analyst declaration explicitly.

Bootstrap requests stop at 1,000,000 replicates and 10,000,000
replicate-by-contribution entries. For Bonferroni intervals, fewer than ten expected
replicates per adjusted tail (`B * (alpha / m) / 2 < 10`) produces
`delta_ci_adjusted_status="insufficient_tail_resamples"`; no normal-SE interval replaces
it. Numeric overflow raises with a request to rescale. These computational guards
do not certify coverage for heavy-tailed or dependent data.

